# The taxpayer's own purchase register — what they claim.
#
# **Eight invoices, planted so that each finding rule has both a case that
# fires and a case that must not.** A fixture where every row is a problem
# proves only that a rule matches everything; the clean rows are what prove it
# discriminates.
#
#   INV-1001  matches GSTR-2B exactly                     → nothing
#   INV-1002  tax and taxable value both differ           → AmountMismatch
#   INV-1003  never filed by the supplier                 → PotentialMismatch
#   INV-1004  GSTIN transposed (…1ZM vs …1MZ)             → GstinTransposition
#   INV-1005  filed, but credit reported unavailable      → ITCNotAvailable
#   INV-1006  filed, but flagged as reverse-charge        → Reversed
#   INV-1007  supplier filed it, no 2B holds it           → Gstr1NotIn2b
#   INV-1009  reverse-charge, supplier files nothing      → nothing
#   INV-1010  matched, goods received the next month      → GoodsReceiptTiming
#   INV-1011  matched, goods received within the period   → nothing
#   INV-2001  2020 invoice, 5% delta, cap was 10%         → nothing
#   INV-2002  2020 invoice, 20% delta, cap was 10%        → AmountMismatch
#
# …and one invoice that is deliberately **not** here at all: INV-1008 exists
# in GSTR-1 and in GSTR-2B with no register row, which is what `MissingInBooks`
# fires on. An absence cannot be planted in the file it is absent from, so it
# is written up here instead of being discovered as a surprise.
#
# The last two are the ones worth reading twice. Same rule, same query,
# different answer — because `law/rule-36-4.ttl` says the cap in force in
# July 2020 was 10% and the cap in force in July 2026 is nil. If the
# percentage were a constant in the query, INV-2001 would be a false finding
# and a reviewer would be dismissing a compliant invoice every month.
#
# **`gst:Supplier` is a real subject here, not a literal on the invoice —
# Epic 105c (`plans/105c-gst-causal-graph.md`).** INV-1004's transposed GSTIN
# is not a typo in this fixture: it is a *second, distinct* Supplier subject,
# because that is exactly what a transposition means at the graph level — two
# nodes an ngram similarity band suspects are one entity, not yet merged.

@prefix gst: <https://graph-owl.dev/packs/gst#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .

gst:supplier-27AABCU9603R1ZM rdf:type gst:Supplier ;
    gst:supplierGstin "27AABCU9603R1ZM" .

gst:supplier-29AACCG0527D1Z8 rdf:type gst:Supplier ;
    gst:supplierGstin "29AACCG0527D1Z8" .

# The two characters at the end are swapped against what the supplier
# actually filed under — a different subject, deliberately, from
# gst:supplier-27AABCU9603R1ZM above.
gst:supplier-27AABCU9603R1MZ rdf:type gst:Supplier ;
    gst:supplierGstin "27AABCU9603R1MZ" .

gst:pr-INV-1001 rdf:type gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
    gst:issuedBy      gst:supplier-27AABCU9603R1ZM ;
    gst:invoiceNumber "INV-1001" ;
    gst:invoiceDate   "2026-07-04" ;
    gst:taxableValue  "100000.00" ;
    gst:taxAmount     "18000.00" ;
    gst:period        "2026-07" .

gst:pr-INV-1002 rdf:type gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
    gst:issuedBy      gst:supplier-27AABCU9603R1ZM ;
    gst:invoiceNumber "INV-1002" ;
    gst:invoiceDate   "2026-07-09" ;
    gst:taxableValue  "100000.00" ;
    gst:taxAmount     "18000.00" ;
    gst:period        "2026-07" .

gst:pr-INV-1003 rdf:type gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
    gst:issuedBy      gst:supplier-29AACCG0527D1Z8 ;
    gst:invoiceNumber "INV-1003" ;
    gst:invoiceDate   "2026-07-15" ;
    gst:taxableValue  "250000.00" ;
    gst:taxAmount     "45000.00" ;
    gst:period        "2026-07" .

gst:pr-INV-1004 rdf:type gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
    gst:issuedBy      gst:supplier-27AABCU9603R1MZ ;
    gst:invoiceNumber "INV-1004" ;
    gst:invoiceDate   "2026-07-21" ;
    gst:taxableValue  "50000.00" ;
    gst:taxAmount     "9000.00" ;
    gst:period        "2026-07" .

gst:pr-INV-1005 rdf:type gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
    gst:issuedBy      gst:supplier-27AABCU9603R1ZM ;
    gst:invoiceNumber "INV-1005" ;
    gst:invoiceDate   "2026-07-24" ;
    gst:taxableValue  "40000.00" ;
    gst:taxAmount     "7200.00" ;
    gst:period        "2026-07" .

gst:pr-INV-1006 rdf:type gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
    gst:issuedBy      gst:supplier-27AABCU9603R1ZM ;
    gst:invoiceNumber "INV-1006" ;
    gst:invoiceDate   "2026-07-26" ;
    gst:taxableValue  "60000.00" ;
    gst:taxAmount     "10800.00" ;
    gst:period        "2026-07" .

# The four rows Plan 108 adds, each planted for one of the new rules.

# Declared by the supplier on 18 August and in no GSTR-2B this pack holds.
# Fires `Gstr1NotIn2b` and, deliberately, *not* `PotentialMismatch` — the
# vaguer rule stands down once the graph can say which of its two causes
# applies. See `queries/missing-in-gstr2b.sparql`.
gst:pr-INV-1007 rdf:type gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
    gst:issuedBy      gst:supplier-29AACCG0527D1Z8 ;
    gst:invoiceNumber "INV-1007" ;
    gst:invoiceDate   "2026-07-28" ;
    gst:taxableValue  "30000.00" ;
    gst:taxAmount     "5400.00" ;
    gst:period        "2026-07" .

# **The negative case `SupplierNotFiled` exists to not fire on.** A
# reverse-charge purchase: the recipient self-assesses the tax, so there is no
# supplier-side GSTR-1 line to wait for and its absence means nothing is
# wrong. Without this row the rule would look correct while firing on every
# RCM invoice a real taxpayer holds.
gst:pr-INV-1009 rdf:type gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
    gst:issuedBy      gst:supplier-29AACCG0527D1Z8 ;
    gst:invoiceNumber "INV-1009" ;
    gst:invoiceDate   "2026-07-30" ;
    gst:taxableValue  "25000.00" ;
    gst:taxAmount     "4500.00" ;
    gst:reverseCharge "Y" ;
    gst:period        "2026-07" .

# Matched everywhere — books, GSTR-1 and August's 2B all agree — and still not
# claimable in August, because the goods arrived on 4 September. Every
# document check passes; `GoodsReceiptTiming` is the only rule that catches
# it, and it is the reason Section 16(2)(b) is a separate condition.
gst:pr-INV-1010 rdf:type gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
    gst:issuedBy      gst:supplier-27AABCU9603R1ZM ;
    gst:invoiceNumber "INV-1010" ;
    gst:invoiceDate   "2026-08-30" ;
    gst:taxableValue  "20000.00" ;
    gst:taxAmount     "3600.00" ;
    gst:period        "2026-08" .

# The same shape with the goods received *inside* the period — the row that
# proves the rule reads the receipt date rather than merely the presence of a
# receipt.
gst:pr-INV-1011 rdf:type gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
    gst:issuedBy      gst:supplier-27AABCU9603R1ZM ;
    gst:invoiceNumber "INV-1011" ;
    gst:invoiceDate   "2026-08-12" ;
    gst:taxableValue  "15000.00" ;
    gst:taxAmount     "2700.00" ;
    gst:period        "2026-08" .

gst:pr-INV-2001 rdf:type gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
    gst:issuedBy      gst:supplier-27AABCU9603R1ZM ;
    gst:invoiceNumber "INV-2001" ;
    gst:invoiceDate   "2020-07-10" ;
    gst:taxableValue  "100000.00" ;
    gst:taxAmount     "18000.00" ;
    gst:period        "2020-07" .

gst:pr-INV-2002 rdf:type gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
    gst:issuedBy      gst:supplier-27AABCU9603R1ZM ;
    gst:invoiceNumber "INV-2002" ;
    gst:invoiceDate   "2020-07-12" ;
    gst:taxableValue  "100000.00" ;
    gst:taxAmount     "18000.00" ;
    gst:period        "2020-07" .
